WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.440
- I was invited by YouTube

00:00:01.440 --> 00:00:04.200
to interview some politicians
and business leaders

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:07.140
who were attending the APEC
Summit in San Francisco,

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:10.500
and I showed up with a pile
of exams that I gave them

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:13.350
as a way to hear their
thoughts on education.

00:00:13.350 --> 00:00:14.760
So here's what they had to say.

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:18.004
I actually have here this
year's JEE exam from India.

00:00:18.004 --> 00:00:19.250
- Oh my God. Jesus.

00:00:19.250 --> 00:00:21.660
- If I could give that
to you just for a second.

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:23.367
Don't worry, I won't ask
you to solve any problems.

00:00:23.367 --> 00:00:24.390
- Seriously?

00:00:24.390 --> 00:00:26.520
- Well, speaking of Australian education,

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:28.530
I have here just for a little bit of fun

00:00:28.530 --> 00:00:31.767
the latest New South Wales economics exam,

00:00:31.767 --> 00:00:33.240
the HSC, Higher School.

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:35.310
So I wanted to just hand it to you.

00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:36.143
- Okay.

00:00:36.143 --> 00:00:37.710
- As an example of the kind of things

00:00:37.710 --> 00:00:40.470
that stress students out
in the US at the moment.

00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:44.850
And just see if it brought
up any, I guess, emotions

00:00:44.850 --> 00:00:46.380
of what it was like to sit an exam

00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:48.570
or it brings back any stress in any way

00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:51.600
or any other thoughts and
see if it jogs any memories

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:53.280
of your time at school.

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:54.270
- It does.

00:00:54.270 --> 00:00:57.120
- Is this the IIT Joint Entrance Exam or?

00:00:57.120 --> 00:00:57.953
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.
- Okay.

00:00:57.953 --> 00:01:00.330
So I took a bunch of exams
to graduate from high school,

00:01:00.330 --> 00:01:05.193
but these types of questions
do bring back a lot of memory.

00:01:07.530 --> 00:01:11.460
- It is stressful to
look at these problems,

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:16.460
but I do think it goes back
to taking a deep breath

00:01:17.190 --> 00:01:22.190
and what are the tools that
I can use to find the answer?

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:24.360
- I've sat a few of these over the years.

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:25.560
I did high school economics.

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:29.130
The textbooks in the Queensland
Education system at the time

00:01:29.130 --> 00:01:31.140
were written by Brown.

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:31.973
And you know,

00:01:31.973 --> 00:01:35.160
how we managed to navigate
the global financial crisis

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:36.360
without going into recession.

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:39.240
I put that down to a Queensland
high school education

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:41.970
using Browns, the textbooks
on macro and micro.

00:01:41.970 --> 00:01:44.220
But these are fairly standard questions.

00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:47.670
No, they don't excite anxiety in me

00:01:47.670 --> 00:01:50.493
of any particular order of magnitude,

00:01:51.510 --> 00:01:55.503
but I think normally in
preparing for anything,

00:01:56.580 --> 00:01:58.800
there's going to be a level of anxiety

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:02.010
in making sure you've done your homework.

00:02:02.010 --> 00:02:03.630
- It's amazing that you found that though.

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:05.490
- It's a very difficult one. I'd say that.

00:02:05.490 --> 00:02:06.480
Yeah, very challenging.

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:09.900
I wouldn't pass this without
doing a lot more study myself,

00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:12.870
but exams like this one,
they have a lot of questions

00:02:12.870 --> 00:02:15.780
and it's very high
stakes, very competitive.

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:19.410
Often math exams are testing memorization

00:02:19.410 --> 00:02:22.260
or things that perhaps don't seem

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:23.700
like they would ever be practical.

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:25.740
Do you think, if you
can remember back to any

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:29.688
of the math exams you took,
did those prepare you well

00:02:29.688 --> 00:02:32.190
for your job now or for the real world?

00:02:32.190 --> 00:02:34.650
- What I can tell you
from my own experience,

00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:37.620
and it's interesting, I
had my own experience.

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:40.920
Obviously, my wife and I talk
about our own high school

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:42.060
and college experiences,

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:44.580
and then we see it in our children.

00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:45.450
It's very different

00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:47.250
and I don't think it's
actually necessarily

00:02:47.250 --> 00:02:49.440
just a pure difference
of educational systems.

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:51.180
I also think the generation,

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:52.710
the decades make a difference too

00:02:52.710 --> 00:02:54.630
in terms of approach to it.

00:02:54.630 --> 00:02:56.760
And I would say that there
is something to be said

00:02:56.760 --> 00:02:58.860
for the ideas that come

00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:00.390
from actually having more of that sort

00:03:00.390 --> 00:03:02.490
of blank canvas approach
as opposed to like,

00:03:02.490 --> 00:03:05.190
here's like the methodology
that you have to go through.

00:03:05.190 --> 00:03:07.500
Not having that time pressure also.

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:10.260
And so I think I would say
that it's probably a balance,

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:11.550
but I see the value in sort

00:03:11.550 --> 00:03:12.960
of that more open-ended approach.

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:15.180
- I think balance is what we're doing.

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:17.700
We're walking the line
between hindering students

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:20.010
and crushing their soul
with exams like this

00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:22.440
and actually helping
them learn effectively.

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:23.760
- Right, actually getting the substance

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:25.050
is important as well.

00:03:25.050 --> 00:03:26.730
- Memorizing, remembering,

00:03:26.730 --> 00:03:28.710
that aspect helps me really well right now

00:03:28.710 --> 00:03:30.600
as a blind diplomat, right?

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:33.570
I have to remember a lot in my mind

00:03:33.570 --> 00:03:35.910
when I'm entering into giving a speech

00:03:35.910 --> 00:03:37.410
or a talk or dialogue.

00:03:37.410 --> 00:03:41.490
That thinking in math actually
has translated into my work.

00:03:41.490 --> 00:03:44.820
- Education for me has been
a preparation for life.

00:03:44.820 --> 00:03:48.300
There's very little that I've studied,

00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:50.580
I should say, in Chinese language

00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:54.150
and Chinese history that I don't use

00:03:54.150 --> 00:03:56.160
on an almost daily basis

00:03:56.160 --> 00:03:58.320
because of where China
is in the world today.

00:03:58.320 --> 00:04:00.120
Like this morning, I
was doing an interview

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:04.173
about Xi Jinping's summit
with President Biden.

00:04:05.370 --> 00:04:06.450
Look, asked my staff

00:04:06.450 --> 00:04:09.870
to find me the Chinese
readout of the meeting.

00:04:09.870 --> 00:04:11.010
We couldn't find it quickly,

00:04:11.010 --> 00:04:12.660
so I was reading the Chinese text

00:04:13.841 --> 00:04:16.380
and to see whether certain
word usages had changed

00:04:16.380 --> 00:04:18.930
or not from previous Chinese statements.

00:04:18.930 --> 00:04:21.000
That's just one example of the work I do

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:23.250
where you draw directly on
what I learned back then.

00:04:23.250 --> 00:04:24.660
- Have you noticed a difference

00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:27.813
in how different countries
treat education maybe?

00:04:29.070 --> 00:04:33.330
- Well, I've spent actually
most of my adult life in Asia.

00:04:33.330 --> 00:04:35.580
My kids were with me.

00:04:35.580 --> 00:04:39.150
So they lived and went to school in China

00:04:39.150 --> 00:04:41.820
and then South Africa and Singapore

00:04:41.820 --> 00:04:45.030
before we returned to the
US about four years ago.

00:04:45.030 --> 00:04:48.780
So I have seen sort of up close

00:04:48.780 --> 00:04:53.010
how education differs in
some of those countries.

00:04:53.010 --> 00:04:58.010
One thing that is particularly
true in parts of Asia

00:04:58.470 --> 00:05:00.900
that I think is really wonderful

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:03.330
is that the teaching profession

00:05:03.330 --> 00:05:06.570
is really revered and respected.

00:05:06.570 --> 00:05:10.980
The position of being
a teacher is something

00:05:10.980 --> 00:05:13.590
that people really aspire to.

00:05:13.590 --> 00:05:17.070
I wish that we would do more
in the United States also

00:05:17.070 --> 00:05:20.310
to really lift up our
teachers in that way.

00:05:20.310 --> 00:05:21.540
- Yeah, I really agree with that.

00:05:21.540 --> 00:05:24.930
I think the way we treat
teachers means a lot

00:05:24.930 --> 00:05:27.510
and says a lot about what
we value as a society

00:05:27.510 --> 00:05:31.110
and yeah, I've even covered
some exams from India

00:05:31.110 --> 00:05:32.130
and China before,

00:05:32.130 --> 00:05:34.500
and even the people, the students

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:37.920
who top those exams in the
country become mini celebrities

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:39.510
for a while there, you know?

00:05:39.510 --> 00:05:41.190
This person has got the highest score.

00:05:41.190 --> 00:05:42.240
Everyone knows their name.

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:44.490
So I think that that's a culture

00:05:44.490 --> 00:05:47.520
that I think can help foster learning

00:05:47.520 --> 00:05:50.100
and foster people wanting to do well.

00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:52.050
So you've studied in
more than just Australia.

00:05:52.050 --> 00:05:53.820
You went on to Oxford and elsewhere

00:05:53.820 --> 00:05:56.370
and you've been all around
the world through your jobs,

00:05:56.370 --> 00:05:58.380
but have you seen a difference

00:05:58.380 --> 00:06:02.130
in how perhaps different
countries treat education?

00:06:02.130 --> 00:06:04.470
- Well, demonstrably so.

00:06:04.470 --> 00:06:08.160
Each of them has their
own culture of learning,

00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:12.330
but I think there is certainly a tradition

00:06:12.330 --> 00:06:16.530
of academic rigor about a
college-level education here.

00:06:16.530 --> 00:06:19.440
There is a greater emphasis
on the quantitative,

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:21.964
even within the social
sciences than you see

00:06:21.964 --> 00:06:25.680
in the Anglo-Australian universities.

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:27.060
- It's my understanding that you did a bit

00:06:27.060 --> 00:06:30.330
of your education in the
US and a bit in India.

00:06:30.330 --> 00:06:31.770
So I'd love to ask you,

00:06:31.770 --> 00:06:34.260
how did those educational
experiences differ?

00:06:34.260 --> 00:06:36.120
Did you find that those two countries

00:06:36.120 --> 00:06:38.340
treated education differently?

00:06:38.340 --> 00:06:40.410
- That's a really interesting question.

00:06:40.410 --> 00:06:42.270
Probably one of the most
interesting questions

00:06:42.270 --> 00:06:43.103
I've ever gotten.

00:06:43.103 --> 00:06:46.380
It was a bit of a shock for me moving

00:06:46.380 --> 00:06:48.240
from Michigan to India.

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:50.190
Part of this probably had
to do with my interest,

00:06:50.190 --> 00:06:52.920
but it was very science and math focused.

00:06:52.920 --> 00:06:55.440
That's what my interests
also happened to be.

00:06:55.440 --> 00:06:56.700
So let me just put it this way.

00:06:56.700 --> 00:06:59.340
By the time I got done
with high school in India,

00:06:59.340 --> 00:07:02.670
I felt very, very prepared for college.

00:07:02.670 --> 00:07:05.190
I came out actually right here to Stanford

00:07:05.190 --> 00:07:08.550
for college, and I was a
very well prepared freshman,

00:07:08.550 --> 00:07:09.870
let's put it that way.

00:07:09.870 --> 00:07:11.400
The Indian system's pretty intense.

00:07:11.400 --> 00:07:14.310
I had a conversation with a
friend of mine the other day

00:07:14.310 --> 00:07:16.830
and we were just talking about
all the textbooks that we had

00:07:16.830 --> 00:07:18.663
to use back in the day.

00:07:19.860 --> 00:07:21.720
And so you said you're physics, right?

00:07:21.720 --> 00:07:25.440
So like Resnick and Halliday,
like the pages of those books

00:07:25.440 --> 00:07:29.370
are still seared in my
brain from my time in India.

00:07:29.370 --> 00:07:30.660
- All right, as a last question

00:07:30.660 --> 00:07:33.149
before I let you go,
do you have any advice

00:07:33.149 --> 00:07:36.180
for students perhaps taking
their exams at the moment

00:07:36.180 --> 00:07:39.090
on how to handle stress and
what they're going through?

00:07:39.090 --> 00:07:42.360
- For me, one thing was
confidence is a big part of it,

00:07:42.360 --> 00:07:44.520
but also understanding that school

00:07:44.520 --> 00:07:46.560
might be the most
important thing right now,

00:07:46.560 --> 00:07:48.120
but later on in life,

00:07:48.120 --> 00:07:50.550
every single experience
you have that you're taking

00:07:50.550 --> 00:07:53.910
through exams in schools will
be an amazing learned lesson

00:07:53.910 --> 00:07:54.930
for later on in life.

00:07:54.930 --> 00:07:56.640
So things turn out for the best

00:07:56.640 --> 00:08:00.330
and always try to choose
a path, educational path

00:08:00.330 --> 00:08:01.440
that's aligned to your interests

00:08:01.440 --> 00:08:02.760
and your value and your potential.

00:08:02.760 --> 00:08:04.080
That will make a huge difference.

00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:05.580
- Well, thank you very
much for talking with me.

00:08:05.580 --> 00:08:06.990
- Yeah, I enjoyed the conversation.

00:08:06.990 --> 00:08:07.823
- Happy to do it.

00:08:07.823 --> 00:08:10.565
- And I heard a rumor that
you wanted to touch my hair.

00:08:10.565 --> 00:08:12.284
- Oh my God, yes.
- So I'll pass it over to you.

00:08:12.284 --> 00:08:13.951
- Oh my God, you passed it over to me.

00:08:13.951 --> 00:08:14.814
- Oh wow, it's so long

00:08:14.814 --> 00:08:17.100
- So it's just below my knees. Yeah.

00:08:17.100 --> 00:08:18.090
Thank you for watching

00:08:18.090 --> 00:08:20.820
and thank you to my Patreon supporters.

00:08:20.820 --> 00:08:21.870
A special shout out

00:08:21.870 --> 00:08:25.920
to today's Patron Cats of
the Day, Grape and Ditto.

00:08:25.920 --> 00:08:28.587
(lively music)

